= Package examples

Notist.toml installs widgets and mermaid from local directories. Each package has one lib.notc; browser resources live under components/. The panel directory includes a relative style.js import, while badge is a single JS file.

== Static page

```sh
cargo run -- check examples/plugins/document.not
cargo run -- html examples/plugins/document.not --out-dir target/plugins-demo
python3 -m http.server
```

Open /target/plugins-demo/ through the HTTP server. The page contains nested panel elements, an i64 badge count and a Mermaid diagram. Component authors default-export classes; the host generates components.js registrations and copies used resources.

The Mermaid component imports a pinned browser module from jsDelivr. Its rendering API follows [Mermaid usage](https://mermaid.js.org/config/usage). This example requires network access; a package can distribute its own browser dependencies for offline use.

== Web preview

Build the existing browser analyzer with wasm-bindgen-cli 0.2.129, matching the Rust dependency:

```sh
cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version 0.2.129 --locked
./web/build.sh
python3 -m http.server
```

Open /web/ and press 载入项目 with ../examples/plugins/Notist.toml. Configuration and declaration sources are supplied to the pure WASM APIs; browser component modules load in the preview iframe. Select a .notc source path to inspect declarations and diagnostics.

== Verification

```sh
cargo test --workspace --all-features
node --test web/tests/protocol.test.mjs
```

web/tests/browser.cjs additionally verifies static rendering, reconnection, preview and module diagnostics using Playwright. Serve the repository root, build target/plugins-demo and web/pkg first, then run the script with Playwright available on NODE_PATH. NOTIST_TEST_URL changes the HTTP base and NOTIST_CHROMIUM selects an installed browser.
